      {
        "citation": "Van Dover, C. L., Szuts, E. Z., Chamberlain, S. C., & Cann, J. R. (1989). A novel eye in 'eyeless' shrimp from hydrothermal vents of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge. Nature, 337, 458\u2013460.",
        "url": "https://doi.org/10.1038/337458a0",
        "doi": null,
        "supported_claims": "Rimicaris exoculata lacks normal image-forming eyes and possesses a reflective dorsal rhodopsin photoreceptor organ sensitive to faint thermal radiation from ~350\u00b0C vents."
      },
      {
        "citation": "Petersen, J. M. et al. (2011). Hydrogen is an energy source for hydrothermal vent symbioses. Nature, 476, 176\u2013180; Ponsard, J. et al. (2013). Inorganic carbon fixation by chemosynthetic ectosymbionts of Rimicaris exoculata. ISME Journal, 7, 96\u2013109.",
        "url": "https://doi.org/10.1038/nature10325",
        "doi": null,
        "supported_claims": "Chemosynthetic ectosymbiotic bacteria inside Rimicaris gill chambers oxidize hydrogen sulfide and reduced compounds to nourish dense (~2,500/m\u00b2) shrimp swarms without sunlight."
      },
      {
        "citation": "Humphris, S. E. et al. (1995). The internal structure of an active sea-floor massive sulphide deposit (TAG hydrothermal mound, 3,670 m, Mid-Atlantic Ridge). Nature, 377, 713\u2013716; Martin, W. et al. (2008). Hydrothermal vents and the origin of life. Nature Reviews Microbiology, 6, 805\u2013814.",
        "url": "https://doi.org/10.1038/377713a0",
        "doi": null,
        "supported_claims": "At ~3,600 m (~360 bar hydrostatic pressure), ~360\u00b0C hydrothermal fluids remain liquid without boiling, precipitate metal-sulfide chimneys upon mixing with ~2\u00b0C seawater, and serve as a leading (though debated vs. terrestrial hot springs) model for abiogenesis and icy-moon astrobiology."
      }
